.TH "lazy" 3 "Version 0.9.0" "mbedded-cbor" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
lazy \- read a CBOR message in place, step by step
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SS "Classes"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "struct \fBcbor::lazy\fP"
.br
.RI "A position in one top-level item: the owner of the bytes, the bytes and an offset\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "struct \fBcbor::lazy_elements\fP"
.br
.RI "The elements of one array, from \fBcbor::lazy::elements()\fP\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "struct \fBcbor::lazy_entries\fP"
.br
.RI "The pairs of one map, from \fBcbor::lazy::entries()\fP, in the order of the bytes\&. "
.in -1c
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP 
\fR\fBcbor::lazy\fP\fP reads a CBOR message in place\&. It copies no byte of the message\&. Each step reads only the heads up to its child\&. Include \fR<\fBcbor/cbor\&.hpp\fP>\fP\&.

.PP
A \fR\fBcbor::lazy\fP\fP is a position in one top-level item\&. Each member function reads the bytes at the position when it is called\&. A copy of a lazy is a second position in the same bytes\&. Several threads can read one lazy at the same time\&.

.PP
Each step gives a \fRstd::expected\fP with a \fR\fBcbor::error\fP\fP\&. A chain of steps stops at the first error\&.
.SH "MAKE A LAZY"
.PP
\fR\fBcbor::lazy::adopt\fP\fP and \fR\fBcbor::lazy::view\fP\fP make the lazy of the top-level item\&. Neither copies the bytes\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRadopt(std::move(bytes))\fP: \fRbytes\fP is a \fRstd::string\fP\&. It moves into storage that the lazy owns\&. An lvalue, a \fRstd::string_view\fP and a literal do not compile\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRview(owner)\fP: \fRowner\fP is a \fRstd::shared_ptr<std::string const>\fP\&. The lazy reads \fR*owner\fP in place\&. Every result keeps \fRowner\fP alive\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRview(owner, part)\fP: \fRpart\fP is a \fRstd::string_view\fP into memory that \fRowner\fP, a \fRstd::shared_ptr<void const>\fP, keeps alive\&.
.PP

.PP
\fRview\fP throws \fRstd::logic_error\fP for a null \fRowner\fP\&. The form with \fRpart\fP also throws it for an empty \fRowner\fP\&.

.PP
\fRadopt\fP and \fRview\fP read only the first head\&. They skip one leading tag 55799\&. They check no limit\&. They skip a tag 55799 in every width of its argument\&. They do not check that the bytes are one complete item\&. A step finds a missing part, and no step reads a byte after the item\&. The bytes \fR82 01\fP make a lazy, and a read of the second element gives \fRtoo_little_data\fP\&.
.SH "STEPS IN A MAP OR AN ARRAY"
.PP
\fRat\fP gives the lazy of one child\&. \fRoperator[]\fP gives the same result\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRat(key)\fP: the value of the first pair whose key is the text \fRkey\fP\&. A text key does not match a byte string with the same bytes\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRat(cbor::key{n})\fP: the value of the first pair whose key is the integer \fRn\fP\&. A bignum key that holds \fRn\fP matches too\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRat(index)\fP: the element of an array or of a typed array, counted from 0\&.
.PP

.PP
A map with a key twice is no error\&. \fRat\fP gives the first pair\&.

.PP
A key under tag 28 or tag 29 is a tag\&. It matches no text key and no integer key\&. A key under tag 55799 matches as the key without the tag (RFC 8949, section 3\&.4\&.6)\&. A key \fR2(h'01')\fP is the same key as \fR1\fP\&.

.PP
\fRoperator[]\fP does not throw for a missing key or a wrong index\&. The result is a \fRstd::expected\fP, so \fRl["a"]["b"]\fP does not compile\&. To read a chain, use \fR\fBcbor::lazy::at_path()\fP\fP\&.

.PP
A step reads the heads from the start of its map or array to the target, and the head of the target\&. It reads no byte after the head of the target\&. A target that the input does not hold gives \fRtoo_little_data\fP at the step\&. A map or an array that claims more items than the bytes left gives \fRtoo_little_data\fP before the step reads an element\&.
.SH "READ A VALUE"
.PP
\fRget<T>()\fP reads the value at the lazy as \fRT\fP\&. \fRget\fP passes a tag that it does not interpret\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
an integer type, not \fRbool\fP: an integer, or a bignum (tag 2 or 3) that fits \fRT\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdouble\fP: a float of 16, 32 or 64 bits, or an integer or a bignum that a \fRdouble\fP holds exactly\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbool\fP: \fRfalse\fP or \fRtrue\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::nullptr_t\fP: \fRnull\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcbor::simple_value\fP: a simple value, for example \fRundefined\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::string_view\fP: a text string, as a \fR\fBcbor::ref\fP<std::string_view>\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::span<std::byte const>\fP: a byte string, as a \fR\fBcbor::ref\fP<std::span<std::byte const>>\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcbor::typed_array\fP: a typed array (RFC 8746), as a \fR\fBcbor::ref\fP<cbor::typed_array>\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::float128_t\fP, where the compiler has it: an element of a typed array of 128-bit floats\&.
.PP

.PP
A \fR\fBcbor::ref\fP\fP views the bytes and keeps their owner alive\&. Nothing is copied\&.

.PP
The binary16 float \fR1\&.5\fP (\fRf9 3e 00\fP) reads as \fR1\&.5\fP\&. The integer \fR42\fP reads as \fR42\&.0\fP for \fRdouble\fP\&. The bignum \fR2(h'05')\fP reads as \fR5\fP for \fRstd::int64_t\fP\&.
.SH "THE COUNT, THE ELEMENTS AND THE PAIRS"
.PP
\fRsize()\fP gives the count of the head of an array, a map or a typed array\&. It reads only the head\&. It gives a \fRstd::expected\fP of \fRstd::uint64_t\fP\&. \fRempty()\fP gives \fRtrue\fP when the count is 0\&.

.PP
\fRelements()\fP gives the elements of an array, or of a typed array, as a view\&. Each element is a \fRstd::expected<\fBcbor::lazy\fP, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP\&. The view reads one element at each step\&. It is a forward range, a sized range and a borrowed range, so the standard algorithms take it\&. It has no \fRfront()\fP\&. Its \fRsize()\fP gives the count of the head, and it gives exactly that many values\&.

.PP
\fRentries()\fP gives the pairs of a map, in the order of the bytes, as a view\&. Each element is a \fRstd::expected\fP of a pair of lazy values, or of a \fR\fBcbor::error\fP\fP\&. The key is \fRfirst\fP and the value is \fRsecond\fP\&. A key that occurs twice occurs twice in the view\&. The view is a forward range, a sized range and a borrowed range\&.

.PP
An iterator holds the owner of the bytes\&. It stays valid after the lazy and the view end\&. When the iterator cannot step over an element, \fR*it\fP gives \fRtoo_little_data\fP or another error of the bytes\&. Each later position gives that error\&.
.SH "COPY ONE ITEM TO A TREE"
.PP
\fRclone()\fP gives a \fRstd::expected<\fBcbor::owning\fP<\fBcbor::item\fP>, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP\&. It reads the whole item at the lazy, checks every head, and builds one \fR\fBcbor::item\fP\fP for each data item\&. The tree copies each text and byte string\&. It does not view the bytes of the lazy, and it stays valid after the lazy and its owner end\&. The errors of \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP apply\&.

.PP
\fRclone\fP keeps tags 28 and 29 as tag items\&. A tag 55799 inside the top-level item stays a tag item, and \fRtype()\fP gives \fRtag\fP there\&. \fRget\fP, \fRat\fP and a path read through such a tag to its content\&. They do not read through it where it is the content of a tag 2, a tag 3 or a typed array\&. There the content is not a byte string, as \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP reads it\&. \fRclone\fP of a lazy that starts with a tag 55799 gives the content\&.

.PP
A bignum (tag 2 or 3) whose magnitude fits 64 bits is a number in \fRtype()\fP, \fRget<int>\fP, \fRget<double>\fP, a path and a key lookup\&. No read gives it as a tag\&. A larger bignum stays a tag item\&. The library has no type for big numbers and depends on no library for them\&.

.PP
\fRtype()\fP gives a \fRstd::expected<cbor::type, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP, the kind of the value at the lazy\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
A tag 2 or 3 whose magnitude fits 64 bits gives \fRunsigned_integer\fP or \fRnegative_integer\fP (RFC 8949, section 3\&.4\&.3)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A larger bignum gives \fRunsigned_bignum\fP or \fRnegative_bignum\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A typed array (RFC 8746) gives \fRtyped_array\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Every other tag gives \fRtag\fP\&. \fRtype()\fP does not pass the tag\&.
.PP
.SH "ERRORS"
.PP
Each step gives a \fR\fBcbor::error\fP\fP:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRkey_not_found\fP: no pair has the key\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRindex_out_of_bounds\fP: the index is not less than the count\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnot_indexable\fP: a key or an index on a value of the wrong kind; \fRsize()\fP on a value that is not an array, a map or a typed array; \fRelements()\fP on a value that is not an array; \fRentries()\fP on a value that is not a map\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRincorrect_type\fP: \fRget<T>()\fP of another kind of value\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnumber_out_of_range\fP: a number that does not fit \fRT\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtoo_little_data\fP: the bytes end before the item, or a count or a string is longer than the bytes left\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsyntax_error\fP: a head is broken\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRindefinite_length\fP: \fRelements()\fP or \fRentries()\fP of an array or a map of indefinite length, or \fRclone()\fP of such an item\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnesting_depth_exceeded\fP: \fRclone()\fP of an item that nests too deep\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnot_enough_memory\fP: an allocation of the owner in \fRadopt\fP fails\&.
.PP

.PP
A wrong use throws \fRstd::logic_error\fP\&. It throws for a null \fRowner\fP in \fRview\fP, and for an empty \fRowner\fP in the form with \fRpart\fP\&. It throws for each member function of a lazy that holds no top-level item, for example a \fR\fBcbor::lazy\fP{}\fP\&.

.PP
 
.SH "EXAMPLES"
 
.PP
.nf
    // bytes: a std::string with {"name": "ann", "scores": [4, 5, 6]}
    auto const doc = cbor::lazy::adopt(std::move(bytes));
    auto const name = doc\->at("name")\->get<std::string_view>();  // a ref to "ann"
    auto const scores = doc\->at("scores");
    auto const count = scores\->size();                             // 3
    auto const elements = scores\->elements();
    int total = 0;
    for (auto const element : *elements)
        total += element\->get<int>()\&.value_or(0);
    // total: 15
.fi
.PP
 
